## Part 1. Lemma 3: which periodic schedules with frequencies (1/2, 1/4, 1/4) satisfy it

lag_k = position of the stream's k-th service minus k/w. The displayed bound (position j_i reached by (j_i+1)/w_i, j_i counted from 0) needs lag <= 0 for every k; the proof's sentence ('j-th time by (j+1)/w_i') needs lag <= 1/w_i.

| schedule | max lag S (1/w=2) | max lag U (1/w=4) | max lag H (1/w=4) | displayed bound | proof's sentence | scan sure bound 2n0-1 (n0=8,9) | E[q], one mistake at step 0, n=64 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| H, S, S, U | +0 | +0 | -3 | holds | holds | FAILS | 2.000 (bound (0+1)/w_S = 2) |
| H, S, U, S | +0 | -1 | -3 | holds | holds | FAILS | 2.000 (bound (0+1)/w_S = 2) |
| H, U, S, S | +1 | -2 | -3 | FAILS | holds | FAILS | 2.984 (bound (0+1)/w_S = 2) |
| S, H, S, U | -1 | +0 | -2 | holds | holds | holds | 1.000 (bound (0+1)/w_S = 2) |
| S, H, U, S | +0 | -1 | -2 | holds | holds | FAILS | 1.000 (bound (0+1)/w_S = 2) |
| S, S, H, U | -1 | +0 | -1 | holds | holds | holds | 1.000 (bound (0+1)/w_S = 2) |
| S, S, U, H | -1 | -1 | +0 | holds | holds | holds | 1.000 (bound (0+1)/w_S = 2) |
| S, U, H, S | +0 | -2 | -1 | holds | holds | FAILS | 1.000 (bound (0+1)/w_S = 2) |
| S, U, S, H | -1 | -2 | +0 | holds | holds | holds | 1.000 (bound (0+1)/w_S = 2) |
| U, H, S, S | +1 | -3 | -2 | FAILS | holds | FAILS | 2.969 (bound (0+1)/w_S = 2) |
| U, S, H, S | +0 | -3 | -1 | holds | holds | FAILS | 1.984 (bound (0+1)/w_S = 2) |
| U, S, S, H | +0 | -3 | +0 | holds | holds | FAILS | 1.984 (bound (0+1)/w_S = 2) |
| U, U, H, H, S, S, S, S | +3 | -3 | -1 | FAILS | FAILS | FAILS | 4.891 (bound (0+1)/w_S = 2) |

Reading: only schedules whose every stream has lag <= 0 (each stream served k times by position k/w, i.e. discrepancy below one from the start) satisfy the displayed bound. S, U, S, H is one. 'Bounded discrepancy' alone gives k/w + O(period).

## Part 2. Notebook-level (A4') with the output rule 'first passing claim'

T = 10 problems, class {P} (K = 0). Notebook-level (A4') holds on every final notebook: True. S_V holds and S* holds on every run.
Outputs (first passing claim) that are wrong: 10 of 10. Accepted runs failing S*: 0.
So Theorem 5(a) ('under (A4') every output satisfies S*') fails for the output artifact, and 5(f)'s 'failed outputs are exactly the accepted runs that fail S*' is false: 10 wrong outputs against a bound of 0. With (A4') stated per claim ('every claim V passes in the imitator's final notebooks is a solution'), c1 violates (A4'), as it should.

